WEBVTT

1
00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:15.112
Jacob sat with the Christmas present from his grandparents on his lap while the whole family looked on.

2
00:00:15.132 --> 00:00:20.473
"Let me guess," he said, slowly unwrapping the box with a forced smile.

3
00:00:20.493 --> 00:00:22.894
"Socks again?"

4
00:00:24.014 --> 00:00:27.075
He opened the box and found a piece of paper instead.

5
00:00:28.215 --> 00:00:31.613
It was a handwritten note that read: "Merry Christmas!

6
00:00:32.093 --> 00:00:37.257
You, Jacob Price, are the owner of five shares of Orange Software Company's stock."

7
00:00:38.198 --> 00:00:40.440
"Instead of socks, we got you stock!"

8
00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:41.621
Grandpa said.

9
00:00:41.641 --> 00:00:44.103
"Thank you, Grandpa and Grandma!"

10
00:00:44.483 --> 00:00:45.244
Jacob said.

11
00:00:45.264 --> 00:00:47.366
"I love Orange's games!

12
00:00:48.407 --> 00:00:50.268
Does this mean I'm going to be rich?"

13
00:00:51.829 --> 00:00:52.830
Everyone laughed.

14
00:00:57.894 --> 00:00:59.396
with me," Grandpa said.

15
00:01:00.396 --> 00:01:05.120
He led Jacob into his office as the rest of the family opened their last presents.

16
00:01:09.743 --> 00:01:13.045
"Your grandmother and I wanted to give you something different this year.

17
00:01:14.486 --> 00:01:17.909
Your parents told us not to give you money since you'd just spend it."

18
00:01:18.769 --> 00:01:20.331
Jacob grinned sheepishly.

19
00:01:20.351 --> 00:01:23.953
"What else can you do with money besides save or spend it?"

20
00:01:25.074 --> 00:01:27.456
"You can invest it," Grandpa said.

21
00:01:28.628 --> 00:01:32.371
"Your present this year is an introduction to investing in the stock market."

22
00:01:33.472 --> 00:01:35.254
Jacob looked down at the note again.

23
00:01:35.274 --> 00:01:39.457
"I'm an owner—but I don't own the whole company, right?"

24
00:01:40.879 --> 00:01:41.979
Grandpa smiled.

25
00:01:41.999 --> 00:01:45.322
"Right. You own stock in the company.

26
00:01:46.383 --> 00:01:48.245
Stock is ownership in the company.

27
00:01:49.206 --> 00:01:50.647
You own five shares.

28
00:01:51.728 --> 00:01:53.369
A share is a unit of ownership.

29
00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:56.292
Companies sell stock to raise money."

30
00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.082
"Don't they make enough money with their products?"

31
00:02:01.462 --> 00:02:02.243
Jacob asked.

32
00:02:03.304 --> 00:02:07.727
"Orange Software has a new game coming out soon—it's going to sell millions of copies!"

33
00:02:07.747 --> 00:02:14.673
"Orange makes a lot of money, but companies always need more money to help them expand or compete

34
00:02:14.693 --> 00:02:15.534
with other companies.

35
00:02:16.435 --> 00:02:21.339
They can use the money from selling stock to develop new products or improve existing ones.

36
00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:26.724
They can also use the money to hire employees or cover other business costs."

37
00:02:28.672 --> 00:02:29.493
Jacob nodded.

38
00:02:29.513 --> 00:02:32.474
"Now I get why companies sell stock.

39
00:02:33.455 --> 00:02:35.316
But how do people make money buying it?"

40
00:02:35.336 --> 00:02:40.119
"It's an investment—using money to make more money," Grandpa said.

41
00:02:40.139 --> 00:02:46.382
"If a company does well, and its profits go up, stock owners share in the profits.

42
00:02:47.403 --> 00:02:50.525
Then they can sell their stock at a higher price than they first paid."

43
00:02:50.545 --> 00:02:53.186
"Cool," Jacob said.

44
00:02:53.206 --> 00:02:55.568
"Money that grows!"

45
00:02:57.524 --> 00:03:00.746
"It can also go the other way," Grandpa warned.

46
00:03:00.766 --> 00:03:03.348
"Investing is often risky.

47
00:03:04.349 --> 00:03:07.211
If the company loses money, the stock price goes down.

48
00:03:08.532 --> 00:03:10.813
There's no guarantee which way it will go."

49
00:03:15.297 --> 00:03:20.100
Jacob didn't think much about his present until one morning a few weeks later.

50
00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:27.929
"Orange's latest game has already sold thousands of copies since its release," Dad said from behind a newspaper.

51
00:03:27.949 --> 00:03:31.012
"The stock price increased 7 percent."

52
00:03:31.032 --> 00:03:33.734
"Does that mean I'm making money?"

53
00:03:33.754 --> 00:03:42.200
"Yep." Dad showed Jacob the stock market section of the paper, pointing to Orange Software's listing.

54
00:03:46.703 --> 00:03:47.463
Jacob said.

55
00:03:47.483 --> 00:03:49.164
"I made $17.50!"

56
00:03:49.184 --> 00:04:00.311
Soon Jacob began paying more attention to the stock market, checking Orange Software's stock every day.

57
00:04:01.091 --> 00:04:04.992
Some days it increased a little, while other days it increased a lot.

58
00:04:05.913 --> 00:04:08.654
Then there were days when the stock price dropped a little.

59
00:04:09.634 --> 00:04:15.196
And one day, after a serious glitch was discovered in a game, the price dropped a lot.

60
00:04:16.337 --> 00:04:19.238
When Grandpa called, Jacob talked to him about it.

61
00:04:19.258 --> 00:04:23.620
"Don't worry too much about little ups or downs," Grandpa said.

62
00:04:24.672 --> 00:04:27.293
"It's better to think in the long term with investing.

63
00:04:28.174 --> 00:04:30.835
Stock prices go up and down every day.

64
00:04:31.735 --> 00:04:35.737
Focus on whether a stock does well or not over a longer period of time."

65
00:04:36.677 --> 00:04:39.618
For the next year, Jacob tracked Orange's stock.

66
00:04:41.279 --> 00:04:44.360
By the following Christmas, it had doubled in price.

67
00:04:45.361 --> 00:04:48.642
Jacob had also been working on saving a lot of his allowance.

68
00:04:48.662 --> 00:04:53.444
"I'm thinking about buying more stock someday," he said to Grandpa.

69
00:04:55.264 --> 00:04:59.427
"I did lots of research before buying stock in Orange," Grandpa said.

70
00:04:59.447 --> 00:05:03.509
"Big or popular companies aren't always a wise choice.

71
00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:08.152
It's important to read about a company in order to make a smart investment."

72
00:05:08.172 --> 00:05:10.533
"Got it," Jacob said.

73
00:05:11.654 --> 00:05:17.657
After dinner, when it was time to open presents, Jacob reached for Grandpa and Grandma's package first.

74
00:05:17.677 --> 00:05:19.618
"More stock?"

75
00:05:19.998 --> 00:05:22.200
he asked, eagerly tearing into the present.

76
00:05:23.368 --> 00:05:28.292
"Since you've already learned about the stock market and investing Grandpa grinned.

77
00:05:28.312 --> 00:05:33.536
"We went back to—" Jacob laughed.
